# The language tests: those whose expectation the language alone fixes, so
# that another implementation of the language can run them.
#
# A test listed here is a program and what any correct implementation must
# do with it.  If its .exit holds 1, it promises that status and nothing
# else, neither its messages nor anything written before the failure: with
# -c among its .args, that the program is rejected before anything is
# evaluated; without it, only that running the program fails; and where it
# holds several faulty cases, only that one of them was refused.  Any other
# test promises status 0 and the answers its .expected holds, one for each
# query, in the order the program asks them.  An answer is compared as a
# collection of rows whose order does not count but in which a row written
# twice does, and a row as its values, each the value its literal denotes
# rather than the literal's spelling.  What a test writes to the standard
# error, warnings included, is relic's own and no part of the promise.
#
# The .expected is in relic(1)'s text format: one row per line, its values
# written as literals of the language and separated by a space, so that a
# reader takes a row apart as the language's lexer would; and an empty line
# between one query's answer and the next.  A query of no columns that
# holds answers one row of no values, an empty line, so telling the answers
# apart takes each query's number of columns, which the program gives.  No
# test here asks two queries of no columns that answer differently unless
# an answer with rows stands between them, since the empty lines would show
# how many held but not which.  The option -s sorts each answer's rows, and
# -c checks the program without evaluating it, so that nothing is answered.
#
# A listed test runs the program alone, so it is its .dl and nothing beside
# it but an .expected, an .expected-err, an .exit holding 0 or 1, and .args
# holding only -c and -s.  tests/langfiles.sh holds this list to the
# directory in both directions: every test it names must qualify, and every
# test that qualifies must be named, as a line of its own or after `omit`
# with the reason it is left out.  A test is omitted only when what it
# expects, cut to its status and its answers, is not the language's to
# decide.
#
# The names are in byte order, and so are the omissions.

